content:
E31 shows 0 truly orphaned orders — the I2 violations are a backlog being processed, not structurally stuck orders. The requeue_orphaned_orders action is likely not needed.

E32 is very revealing: 564 unresolved approvals, oldest from t=428 (before Raqib's watch at t=300, actually during baseline-adjacent period), callback_due up to t=785. This is a large pile of approvals that haven't been resolved. The oldest at t=428 predates the worker crash (t=588), suggesting the supplier-api issue started earlier — around when invoice-agent v1.1 was deployed at t=411 with a "retry policy update" (E14). That deploy is 99s before the first anomaly at t=510 (E8, orders_fulfilled drop).

Let me check if the approval backlog correlates with the invoice-agent deploy or if supplier-api is genuinely down.

```json
{
  "investigate": [
    {
      "tool": "sql",
      "query": "SELECT COUNT(*) as total, SUM(CASE WHEN requested_at < 510 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) as before_anomaly, SUM(CASE WHEN resolved_at IS NULL AND callback_due < :now THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) as overdue, SUM(CASE WHEN resolved_at IS NOT NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) as resolved FROM approvals",
      "why": "Determine how many approvals were already pending before t=510 (first anomaly) vs after, and how many are overdue — this separates a pre-existing supplier-api failure from a backlog caused by the worker crash or invoice-agent deploy."
    }
  ],
  "hypotheses": [
    {
      "service": "workflow",
      "kind": "external_dependency",
      "mechanism": "supplier-api has been unresponsive since around t=428 (oldest unresolved approval), causing all paid orders to stall waiting for supplier approval and producing 22 I4 violations and 564 pending approvals.",
      "supporting": ["E2", "E8", "E27", "E30", "E32"],
      "refuting": [],
      "explains": ["I4_workflow_progress"],
      "repair_plan": [
        {"action": "rerequest_supplier_approvals", "params": {}}
      ]
    },
    {
      "service": "worker",
      "kind": "runtime_fault",
      "mechanism": "Worker entered a crash-loop at t=588 with no deploy behind it, spiking queue depth to 595 and creating 245 orphaned-order violations; the restart at t=700 resolved the crashes and the backlog is now draining (0 structurally orphaned orders per E31).",
      "supporting": ["E1", "E11", "E12", "E13", "E6"],
      "refuting": ["E31"],
      "explains": ["I2_no_orphaned_orders"],
      "repair_plan": []
    }
  ],
  "summary": "564 supplier approvals have been pending since t=428 pointing to a supplier-api outage as the root cause of I4; the I2 backlog from the worker crash-loop is self-resolving with 0 structurally orphaned orders remaining."
}
```
